comments.body:
Have wrestled with the longlist and, just this once in fifteen years of playing this game, the judges' whittling matched my own so would be happy to see any of them win. It's completely subjective of course but, for what it's worth (and criminally briefly), here's my two pence worth:
- The Disapperers needs an edit, badly;
- May We Feed the King starts as a work of genius but the historical sections don't match the incisive beauty of the curator sections;
- The End of Everything is amazing but half the glory was its Southern British setting and using the clues to plot the characters' journeys so less international appeal than other contenders; 
I'd lose serious amounts of sleep choosing between the remaining three but...
- John of John edges into cliche from time to time but the rest is sheer brilliance, especially the grandma character and the descriptions of the weaving; 
- The Things We Never Say is so gentle, so subtle, so much what we need to hear right now but doesn't push the envelope very far, which leaves 
- poet but debut novelist Luke Kennard's Black Bag, with its outrageous dark humour but deep psychological undertow with my vote. If The Sellout or Vernon God Little can win, surely this satire can too. We'll know by Monday!
